A||||15||88formula||Stupid clutch won't disengage, now what?||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||07-21-2001||01:43 AM||||Got my motor running and everything else on the car works great. The only problem is that the clutch won't fully disengage. I can't figure this one out. I've replaced the master and slave cylinder and went through about two quarts bleeding this crappy clutch. Gravity bleeding seems to be the only way to get all the air out. I'm positive that there is no air in the hydraulic system. I have to stand on the pedal to shift and even then it grinds in reverse and is almost impossible to shift into first at a stoplight. I measured the length that the clutch fork travels at the slave pushrod when the clutch pedal is depressed fully and it travels just under 3/4-inch or 11/16-inch. Is this distance correct or should it travel more? <P>I’ve just about tried everything and I’m out of ideas. Has this happened to you where everything seems to be working hydraulically but the clutch won’t fully disengage? I thought it might be a bent clutch pedal but I have an 88 formula with the steel pedal and I had no problems with the old clutch. In fact the old clutch grabbed near the top of the clutch pedal travel and this one grads almost on the floor.<P>This is so frustrating, I spend I year rebuilding the motor, brakes, and suspension and now that I finally have everything put back together the f___ing clutch wont work right. I’ve spent a week trying to figure this out and I’ve just about given up. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-21-2001||01:56 AM||||Have you checked the shift lever on the tranny? I don't know about the 88's, but the lever on my 85 broke and it wouldn't disengage. (The lever that I'm talking about is the one that the clutch slave cylinder pushes on that twists the clutch forks.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||07-21-2001||02:19 AM||||Yeah I removed it when I replaced the fork bushings and seal. There was nothing wrong with it. I removed it two other times just to inspect it and make sure it was not bent. <P>I think I’m going replace the clutch pedal, what the hell I’ve already spent over $200 trying to fix this shitty hydraulic system. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||07-21-2001||05:12 AM||||You should have closer to an inch of travel for the clutch to engage properly.<P>You say you replaced the slave and master cylinders.......did you use GM parts or aftermarket?  Believe it or not it makes a difference.  Only use new GM clutch hydraulics.....you can bleed the system n a few pumps.  It seems you can never get all of the air out of the aftermarket stuff.<P>Since it is engaging right off of the floor it is definitley a hydraulic issue.  <P>Good luck.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||PJ||07-21-2001||10:57 AM||||Mrfiero is right. I replaced my clutch master with a brand new Wagner I had lying around the shop. After about 45min. of trying to bleed the piece of crap I got a new OEM one from the dealer down the block. Two or three pumps and instant pedal, done. Also if you resurfaced the flywheel it could now be too thin to disengage. Machining moves the pressure plate away from the throwout bearing causing release problems. Generally you can cut the flywheel down once, but any additional resurfacings will cause grief if you don't shim it at the crank.<P>PJ<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||07-21-2001||11:01 AM||||I would have to agree with these guys... It's like when I replaced my alternator.  The problem stayed the same, so I figured it was something else.  After f*cking around trying to figure out what else the problem could be, I ended up just replacing the alternator AGAIN.  Problem solved.  I know that master and slave cylinders are sortof the same thing.  Either they work right, or they don't work at all.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick86GT||07-21-2001||11:09 AM||||I have used several aftermarket slaves and had no trouble w/ them working properly. I use a "one man brake bleeder" to pump fluid through and get most of the air out. Then, with the car on level ground, remove the slave bleeder valve and, behold, several more air bubbles can be seen rising from within.(on my 88GT, which sits a bit lower in the rear, I jack the rear of the car up a couple inches) replace the bleeder and that's it.<P>Not to be simplistic, but when you had the clutch disc out (or installed a new one) are you sure that the disc was re-installed with the appropriate (labeled) side toward the flywheel?  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||07-21-2001||01:24 PM||||The disk only fits one way. I have a new flywheel, clutch, and pressure plate. The clutch operation itself works fine and I have talked to the clutch manufacturer about my situation. The problem lies in the hydraulic system and pedal linkage. The slave pushrod only travels 11/16 of an inch or just over 5/8 of an inch. <P>Just to make sure it wasn't the aftermarket master cylinder I replaced it with the original one, which still works, and I get the same exact results.<P>The slave braket does not move if I watch it and someone pushes on the clutch pedal. <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||07-21-2001||05:39 PM||||Problem solved! <P>What I did was remove the clutch pedal to modify it so that it gives the master cylinder more travel than before. I cut off the pin that the master cylinder rod attaches to and moved it down about 3/8 of an inch. I then welded it back on. The clutch now grabs higher, about 1-1/4 inch off the floor. I can also shift into reverse and first without grinding now. However I think there may be a problem with my shifter cables or linkage. It doesn’t shift as smoothly or crisply as before. <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CLUTCH_PEDAL.JPG">    <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clutch_pedal_1.JPG">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clutch_pedal_2.JPG">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clutch_pedal_3.JPG">   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clutch_pedal_4.JPG">   <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Driven Visions||07-21-2001||06:36 PM||||On my fiero I cut the rod goint to the master cyl and threaded the ends and made a sleeve out of a small piece of pipe and threaded the inside of it<BR>That makes the linkage adjustable <BR>Took care of my problem smooth shifts are now possible<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000010||Driven Visions||07-21-2001||06:38 PM||||On my fiero I cut the rod goint to the master cyl and threaded the ends and made a sleeve out of a small piece of pipe and threaded the inside of it<BR>That makes the linkage adjustable <BR>Took care of my problem smooth shifts are now possible<P>Joe<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||07-22-2001||01:39 AM||||when you had it apart, did u check to see if it had the forged fork or the junk pieced together one. I knew several people including myself that had the pieced one fall apart inside leaving you 'totally like clutchless' LOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||07-22-2001||03:45 AM||||I remember it was one piece and in good shape. It is an 88 car so just about everything is updated. It also only has 50k on it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Brian87egotrip||07-22-2001||09:33 PM||||I just redid the clutch master cylinder and slave on my GT got good pedal and the next morning there was no pedal. I used oem parts. I took it to a local shop to bleed it and guess what the new oem master cylinder had a leak. Replaced it now all is good again! ||||reg||6
Z||000014||Scott||07-24-2001||01:27 AM||||I once went through 2 GM master cyls. before I got one that worked correctly!  By the end of that ordeal, I was doubting my mechanical abilities and beginning to think I had entered the twilight zone.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||07-24-2001||04:30 PM||||Two master cylinders, that can get expensive. I’m going to try the threaded adjustable master cylinder pushrod idea on top of what I have already done. The clutch is disengaging about 95-99% now, it just needs a little more help. I can sometimes slip it into first with no problems and other times I have to play with it to get it to go in. If I let it sit in neutral for a minute I can put it in reverse without grinding.   ||||reg||1
